{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization policy will end on Sunday, as a new law, HB4002, recriminalizes possession of small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl and heroin. Those caught will face a choice: charges or treatment, including a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Possession will be a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and imposes harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas like parks. It encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, though this is not mandatory. Supporters argue this allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, while opponents foresee a confusing, inequitable policy landscape.\n\n***\n\nPortland Police Chief Bob Day noted that eligibility for deflection is strictly defined, requiring no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and medical stability. If eligible, officers will contact a county dispatch line to connect the individual with a mobile behavioral health team, which must respond within 30 minutes, or else the person will be jailed.\n\n***\n\nThe shift in policy underscores the challenges in balancing enforcement with support for addicts. As Oregon navigates this change, the impact on those struggling with addiction will be closely watched. \nP.P.S. As people of all age groups in this country navigate the complexities of substance use, they must also find time to eat and engage in everyday activities like shopping, all while perhaps wondering how this policy affects the social tongue of their society.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.07976089417934418}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3897145688533783}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.149